// #619 M4a sub-step 4: a bound testbench whose lifecycle `check` phase
// contains a CONCURRENT (temporal) assertion. Lowering routes any assert
// carrying a temporal operator (here `|->`) through the concurrent-property
// path, which mints a per-test PropertyCheckId. Sharing this check once
// would register the property for the FIRST binding test only, so the
// conservative scan must classify the testbench UNSAFE and fall back to the
// historical per-test inlining — keeping the trace identical to v1 under
// both codegens. Two impls bind it.
//
// This is the fixture that catches the earlier soundness bug: a purely
// syntactic `named`/`property_kw` check would have deemed `assert a |-> b`
// SAFE and shared it. The authoritative `is_concurrent_assertion` predicate
// classifies it correctly.

testbench ConcurrentAssertTb
    dut : Top

    setup
        dut.rst = 1
        dut.en = 0
        wait 1 cycle
        dut.rst = 0
    end setup

    check
        // Temporal implication (tautology: whenever `en` held, `en` held) —
        // a CONCURRENT assertion, evaluated per primary-clock edge.
        assert dut.en |-> dut.en
    end check
end testbench ConcurrentAssertTb

impl ConcurrentAssertA for ConcurrentAssertTb
    run
        dut.en = 1
        wait 2 cycles
        dut.en = 0
    end run
end impl ConcurrentAssertA

impl ConcurrentAssertB for ConcurrentAssertTb
    run
        dut.en = 1
        wait 4 cycles
        dut.en = 0
    end run
end impl ConcurrentAssertB
